WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.307
This is OPI, an off-policy method that lets a learner compose several imperfect oracles.

2
00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:08.867
First, the setting.

3
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.564
Imagine a route through a highway, a city, and rural roads.

4
00:00:13.844 --> 00:00:19.092
We have three oracles: a highway expert, a city expert, and a rural expert.

5
00:00:19.372 --> 00:00:22.508
Each one is good only on its own part of the route.

6
00:00:22.788 --> 00:00:29.252
In the chart, a darker cell means a higher value: how well that policy does from that state on.

7
00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:34.695
We want a learner that matches or beats every oracle, with few environment steps.

8
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:36.809
So how can we combine them?

9
00:00:37.709 --> 00:00:41.827
The best-oracle policy runs one oracle for the whole episode.

10
00:00:42.107 --> 00:00:47.419
Max-following does better: it hands each state to the oracle with the highest value.

11
00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:57.640
Max-aggregation goes further: it takes the best action at each state, assuming that from the next state on, we follow whichever oracle is best there.

12
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.395
OPI builds on max-aggregation.

13
00:01:01.295 --> 00:01:06.927
First, we treat the learner and the oracles as actors, so the learner can later take over.

14
00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:11.473
The baseline is V-max, the value of the best actor at each state.

15
00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:20.031
The max-advantage of an action is the gain over V-max from taking that action, then following the best actor at the next state.

16
00:01:20.311 --> 00:01:23.297
But we cannot compute the max-advantage directly.

17
00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:27.653
The expected V-max at the next state is intractable.

18
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:32.840
Instead, each actor has a critic, a learned estimate of that actor's return.

19
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:43.680
Following one fixed actor is never better than following the best actor, so the largest critic value minus V-max is a lower bound on the max-advantage.

20
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.328
We call this bound the proxy advantage.

21
00:01:46.608 --> 00:01:53.477
With exact critics, always taking the action with the highest proxy advantage is no worse than V-max.

22
00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:57.427
OPI uses this proxy advantage in two steps.

23
00:01:58.327 --> 00:02:06.903
In search, the learner and the oracles propose actions, and OPI samples the action to execute by its proxy advantage.

24
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:13.881
In update, the learner imitates the actions stored in a replay buffer, weighted by their proxy advantage.

25
00:02:14.161 --> 00:02:16.956
Then the updated learner joins the next search.

26
00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:18.431
First, search.

27
00:02:19.331 --> 00:02:24.365
At each state, every actor proposes actions, and every critic scores them.

28
00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.781
A critic scores its own actor's actions directly.

29
00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:32.541
For other actors' actions, the critic subtracts an uncertainty penalty.

30
00:02:32.821 --> 00:02:34.827
This is the source-aware score.

31
00:02:35.107 --> 00:02:38.371
OPI then samples an action by these scores.

32
00:02:38.651 --> 00:02:44.539
On this city street, the city expert's action is the most likely, at 65 percent.

33
00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:46.440
Why do we need the penalty?

34
00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.545
Without the penalty, the learner's critic over-values an unfamiliar highway action.

35
00:02:52.825 --> 00:02:58.820
Here, OPI would then pick the highway action on a city street 86 percent of the time.

36
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.636
Next, the update step.

37
00:03:01.536 --> 00:03:07.595
To train the learner, plain behavior cloning would copy every replayed action, good or bad.

38
00:03:07.875 --> 00:03:10.115
Instead, we weight the replayed actions.

39
00:03:11.015 --> 00:03:21.788
GABC, generalized advantage-weighted behavior cloning, weights each action by its proxy advantage, so the learner mostly copies the good ones.

40
00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:24.073
But the learner can do even better.

41
00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:35.320
On top of GABC, a deterministic policy gradient, or DPG, climbs the learner's own critic, and moves the learner beyond the data.

42
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.776
Lambda balances the two losses.

43
00:03:38.056 --> 00:03:40.147
Now, does this work in practice?

44
00:03:41.047 --> 00:03:51.415
We evaluate OPI on manipulation tasks from MetaWorld, locomotion tasks from DeepMind Control, and custom Box2D control tasks.

45
00:03:51.695 --> 00:03:57.860
The baselines include Max-V and Max-Q, which imitate max-following and max-aggregation.

46
00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.697
We report two metrics.

47
00:03:59.977 --> 00:04:05.695
NAUC is the normalized area under the learning curve, so it rewards fast learning.

48
00:04:05.975 --> 00:04:08.535
We also report the final performance.

49
00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.521
First, sparse rewards.

50
00:04:11.421 --> 00:04:20.083
With sparse rewards, OPI has the best NAUC on all four tasks, and one hundred percent final success on each.

51
00:04:20.363 --> 00:04:22.069
What about dense rewards?

52
00:04:22.969 --> 00:04:29.796
With dense rewards, OPI remains competitive, with the best NAUC on two of four tasks.

53
00:04:30.076 --> 00:04:33.255
Next, can OPI beat the best single oracle?

54
00:04:34.155 --> 00:04:34.859
Yes.

55
00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:40.493
OPI beats the best single oracle, even where every oracle scores zero.

56
00:04:40.773 --> 00:04:43.269
Does the learner take over from the oracles?

57
00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:44.873
Yes.

58
00:04:45.153 --> 00:04:53.537
During training, OPI selects its own learner more and more often, so OPI relies less and less on the oracles.

59
00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:55.588
Finally, which parts matter?

60
00:04:56.488 --> 00:05:01.693
We remove one part at a time: source-aware scoring, DPG, or sampling.

61
00:05:01.973 --> 00:05:06.645
Without sampling, OPI always executes the top-scoring candidate.

62
00:05:06.925 --> 00:05:08.931
Each removal lowers performance.

63
00:05:09.211 --> 00:05:10.299
To wrap up.

64
00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:16.553
Next, we plan to extend OPI to real-world robotic manipulation.

65
00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:18.327
Thank you for watching.

